### 3. Security Considerations

#### **Q: What are the main security challenges in IoT and how do you address them?**

**Answer**:

```
CHALLENGE 1: Device Authentication
├─ Problem: How to verify device is legitimate?
├─ Traditional: Passwords (impractical for 1M+ devices)
├─ IoT Solution: Certificate-based authentication (X.509)
│  ├─ Each device has unique certificate
│  ├─ Pre-loaded during manufacturing
│  ├─ Device proves identity via certificate
│  ├─ Can revoke certificates remotely
│  └─ Example: AWS IoT uses client certificates
│
└─ Implementation:
   ```python
   # MQTT connection with certificate
   client.tls_set(
       ca_certs="ca.crt",
       certfile="device.crt",
       keyfile="device.key",
       cert_reqs=mqtt.ssl.CERT_REQUIRED
   )
   ```

CHALLENGE 2: Data Encryption in Transit
├─ Problem: Data vulnerable while traveling over network
├─ Solution: TLS/DTLS encryption
│  ├─ TLS: For TCP connections (MQTT, HTTP)
│  ├─ DTLS: For UDP connections (lightweight IoT)
│  ├─ All data encrypted end-to-end
│  └─ Example: HTTPS (secure HTTP), MQTT over TLS
│
└─ Consideration: Encryption adds 5-10% overhead

CHALLENGE 3: Data Storage Security
├─ Problem: Sensitive data at rest (devices, cloud)
├─ Solution: Encryption + access control
│  ├─ Device storage: Encrypt sensor data locally
│  ├─ Cloud storage: Database encryption
│  ├─ Access: Role-based controls (who can access what)
│  └─ Backup: Encrypted backup storage
│
└─ Example: Health data encrypted with AES-256

CHALLENGE 4: Device Updates/Patching
├─ Problem: Firmware bugs discovered after deployment
├─ Traditional: Manual device replacement (expensive/impractical)
├─ IoT Solution: Over-The-Air (OTA) updates
│  ├─ Push updates to devices remotely
│  ├─ Signature verification (ensure authentic)
│  ├─ Rollback capability (if update fails)
│  └─ Staged deployment (test with few devices first)
│
└─ Example:
   ```
   Device receives update notification
   ├─ Verify signature against trusted CA certificate
   ├─ Download update over HTTPS
   ├─ Run new firmware in sandbox test
   ├─ If successful: Replace old firmware
   └─ If fails: Automatic rollback
   ```

CHALLENGE 5: Limited Device Resources
├─ Problem: IoT devices have limited CPU, memory, power
├─ Solution: Lightweight cryptography
│  ├─ ECC (Elliptic Curve Cryptography):
│  │  ├─ Smaller key size: 256-bit ECC ≈ 2048-bit RSA strength
│  │  ├─ Lower power consumption
│  │  └─ Suitable for embedded systems
│  │
│  ├─ HMAC-SHA256: Lightweight authentication
│  └─ ChaCha20: Efficient stream cipher
│
└─ Trade-off: Security vs Performance
   └─ Use encryption appropriate for device capability

CHALLENGE 6: Botnet & DDoS Prevention
├─ Problem: Compromised IoT devices launch attacks
├─ Example: Mirai botnet (hacked 600,000+ IoT devices in 2016)
├─ Solutions:
│  ├─ Default password removal (no "admin/admin")
│  ├─ Automatic security updates
│  ├─ Network segmentation (IoT devices on separate VLAN)
│  ├─ Rate limiting (block too many requests)
│  └─ Anomaly detection (unusual traffic patterns = alert)
│
└─ Case study: Mirai attack
   ├─ Hacked IoT devices (cameras, routers)
   ├─ Millions of devices sent data simultaneously
   ├─ Knocked major websites offline (DNS service)
   ├─ Lesson: IoT security = critical infrastructure security
   └─ Solution: Must force password changes, security updates
```

