### 4. Scalability & Performance

#### **Q: How do you design an IoT system that handles 1 million connected devices?**

**Answer**:

```
CHALLENGE: 1M devices × 1 message/minute = 16,667 messages/second

SOLUTION 1: Message Broker Clustering
├─ Single broker: Can handle ~10,000 connections
├─ Solution: Cluster of brokers (100+)
├─ Each broker: 10,000 connections
├─ Total: 100 brokers × 10,000 = 1M connections
│
└─ Architecture:
   ┌─────────────────┬─────────────────┬─────────────────┐
   │ Broker Node 1   │ Broker Node 2   │ Broker Node 3   │
   │ 10k connections │ 10k connections │ 10k connections │
   └────────┬────────┴────────┬────────┴────────┬────────┘
            └─────────────────┼─────────────────┘
                    Load Balancer (HAProxy)
                              │
                      ┌───────┴───────┐
                      │ Consumer Apps │
                      └───────────────┘

SOLUTION 2: Partitioning/Sharding
├─ Problem: All messages to single database = bottleneck
├─ Solution: Shard by device ID or region
│
├─ Example: 1M devices, 8 shards
│  ├─ Shard 0: Devices 0-124,999
│  ├─ Shard 1: Devices 125,000-249,999
│  ├─ ... (6 more shards)
│  └─ Each shard: ~125,000 devices, separate database
│
└─ Benefits:
   ├─ Database load: Distributed equally
   ├─ Query latency: Reduced (smaller dataset per query)
   └─ Horizontal scaling: Add shards for more devices

SOLUTION 3: Time-Series Database
├─ Problem: Traditional DB slow for IoT time-series data
├─ Solution: Use time-series specific DB
│  ├─ InfluxDB: Optimized for time-series
│  ├─ Prometheus: Metrics storage
│  ├─ TimescaleDB: PostgreSQL extension
│  └─ Features: Automatic compression, retention policies
│
├─ Example InfluxDB setup:
│  ├─ Retention policy: Keep raw data 7 days
│  ├─ Downsampling: Average data to 1-hour intervals after 7 days
│  ├─ Monthly data: 5-minute averages only
│  └─ Result: 90% storage reduction, queries still fast
│
└─ Performance:
   ├─ Write: 100,000+ data points/second
   ├─ Query: Sub-second response time
   └─ Compression: 10-20x ratio typical

SOLUTION 4: Data Aggregation Pipeline
├─ Problem: 16,667 msg/sec to database is too much
├─ Solution: Aggregate before storage
│
├─ Architecture:
│  Devices → Message Broker (MQTT)
│           ↓
│  Stream Processor (Kafka Streams, Flink)
│  ├─ Aggregate: Sum/Count/Average per minute
│  ├─ Filter: Only store important events
│  ├─ Enrich: Add metadata, location, etc
│           ↓
│  Time-Series Database (writes ~1/60th original volume)
│           ↓
│  Datawarehouse (for historical analysis)
│
├─ Example:
│  Raw: 60,000 individual temperature readings/minute
│  Aggregated: 1,000 summary records/minute (60x reduction)
│  Database writes: 16,667 → 277 per second
│  Storage: 90% reduction
│
└─ Trade-off: Lose granularity but gain scalability

SOLUTION 5: Caching Strategy
├─ Problem: Repeated queries hit database repeatedly
├─ Solution: Cache frequently accessed data
│
├─ Multi-tier caching:
│  ├─ Layer 1: Application cache (Redis)
│  │  └─ Latest sensor reading per device
│  ├─ Layer 2: Database page cache
│  │  └─ Frequently queried time ranges
│  └─ Layer 3: Disk storage
│     └─ Historical data archives
│
├─ Example:
│  User requests: "What's temperature right now?"
│  ├─ Check Redis: 50 microseconds (usually hits)
│  ├─ Miss: Query database: 10ms
│  ├─ Cache result: 1 hour TTL
│  └─ Cost: 99% of queries served from memory
│
└─ Hit rate: 95%+ on recent data

SOLUTION 6: Rate Limiting & Circuit Breakers
├─ Prevent: System overload from bad devices
├─ Example: Device sends 1,000 msg/second (malfunction)
│
├─ Rate limiting:
│  ├─ Per-device limit: 100 msg/minute
│  ├─ Device exceeds: Messages dropped/queued
│  ├─ Alert: Operations team investigates
│  └─ Isolation: Only affects that device
│
└─ Prevents: Cascading failures

COMPLETE ARCHITECTURE (1M devices):
