#### **Q5: What's the difference between push and pull architectures in IoT?**

**Expected Answer:**
```
PUSH ARCHITECTURE (Device → Cloud):
├─ Device initiates sending data
├─ Cloud is passive (receives)
│
├─ Advantages:
│  ├─ Real-time: Data arrives immediately
│  ├─ Efficient for cloud: No polling overhead
│  ├─ Batteries: Device can sleep, wake only to send
│  └─ Common: Most IoT systems use this
│
├─ Disadvantages:
│  ├─ Cloud can't request specific data
│  ├─ Device must have logic when to send
│  └─ Device must be reachable (network issues?)
│
├─ Protocol: MQTT (pub/sub), CoAP
└─ Example: Sensor sends temperature every 5 minutes

PULL ARCHITECTURE (Cloud → Device):
├─ Cloud initiates data requests
├─ Device responds when asked
│
├─ Advantages:
│  ├─ Cloud has control: Request only needed data
│  ├─ Flexibility: Change data requests anytime
│  ├─ Device can be simple: Just respond to requests
│  └─ Works through NAT/firewalls better
│
├─ Disadvantages:
│  ├─ Latency: Data old by time cloud requests
│  ├─ Battery drain: Cloud can keep requesting
│  ├─ Cloud overhead: Must poll all devices
│  └─ Scalability: Polling 1M devices is hard
│
├─ Protocol: HTTP (request-response)
└─ Example: Cloud asks "What's temperature?" device responds

HYBRID APPROACH (Recommended):
├─ Device pushes periodically (e.g., every 5 min)
├─ Cloud can pull for immediate data (emergencies)
├─ Example: Smart home
│  ├─ Normal: Temperature pushed every 5 min
│  ├─ User opens app: Cloud pulls live temp
│  └─ Anomaly detected: Device pushes alert immediately

COMPARISON TABLE:
┌──────────────────┬─────────┬─────────┐
│ Aspect           │ Push    │ Pull    │
├──────────────────┼─────────┼─────────┤
│ Latency          │ Low     │ High    │
│ Cloud overhead   │ Low     │ High    │
│ Battery life     │ Good    │ Bad     │
│ Cloud control    │ Low     │ High    │
│ Scalability      │ High    │ Low     │
│ Real-time        │ Yes     │ No      │
└──────────────────┴─────────┴─────────┘
```

