├─ Contains:
│  ├─ Device public key
│  ├─ Device ID / Serial number
│  ├─ Issuer (trusted CA)
│  └─ Expiration date
│
├─ Authentication flow:
│  1. Device requests connection to broker
│  2. Broker: "Prove you are who you say"
│  3. Device: Sends certificate + signed message
│  4. Broker: Verifies signature with public key
│  5. Broker: "You are authenticated!"
│  6. Connection established
│
├─ Advantages:
│  ├─ Secure: Cryptographically proven identity
│  ├─ No passwords to manage
│  ├─ Can revoke certificates remotely
│  └─ Industry standard
│
├─ Implementation (AWS IoT):
│  ├─ Each device has: certificate, private key
│  ├─ Broker has CA certificate
│  ├─ Python example:
│
│     import paho.mqtt.client as mqtt
│     
│     client = mqtt.Client()
│     client.tls_set(
│         ca_certs="AmazonRootCA1.pem",
│         certfile="device-certificate.pem",
│         keyfile="private-key.pem",
│         cert_reqs=mqtt.ssl.CERT_REQUIRED
│     )
│     client.connect("broker.amazonaws.com", 8883)
│
└─ Timeline: Certificate valid 1-5 years

SOLUTION 2: API Keys (Simpler, Less Secure)
├─ Device sends API key with each request
├─ Broker validates key against stored list
├─ Advantages: Simple to implement
├─ Disadvantages: 
│  ├─ Key compromise = redo all devices
│  ├─ Less secure than certificates
│  └─ Not suitable for large deployments

SOLUTION 3: JWT (JSON Web Tokens)
├─ Device sends JWT token
├─ Broker verifies signature
├─ Can include expiration
├─ Example:
│  {
│    "device_id": "sensor_123",
│    "exp": 1704067200,  // Expires in 24h
│    "iat": 1703980800   // Issued at
│  }
├─ Advantages: Stateless, flexible
└─ Disadvantage: Token must be updated regularly

DEPLOYMENT STRATEGY:

Manufacturing Phase:
├─ Device serial: ABC-123
├─ Generate certificate for this serial
├─ Load into device (secure factory)
├─ Store CA certificate on device
└─ Cost: $0.10-1.00 per device

Field Deployment:
├─ Device connects to broker
├─ Sends certificate
├─ Broker verifies authenticity
├─ Device is connected
└─ Cost: Negligible

Certificate Renewal (Every 3 years):
├─ Old certificate expires
├─ Device requests new certificate
├─ Broker issues new certificate (if device valid)
├─ Device updates local certificate
├─ No downtime
└─ Can be automated

Compromised Device:
├─ Broker revokes certificate
├─ Device can no longer connect
├─ Device cannot be hacked to access network
├─ Replicate device with new certificate
└─ Solution: Automated revocation lists

SECURITY BEST PRACTICES:
├─ Private key never leaves device
├─ Private key never transmitted over network
├─ TLS encryption for all communications
├─ Regular security audits
└─ Monitor for suspicious activity
