#### **Q9: How do you protect IoT data in transit?**

**Expected Answer:**
```
THREAT:
├─ Data travels over internet (potentially visible)
├─ Attacker can intercept messages
├─ Can read sensitive information
├─ Can modify messages
└─ Can replay old messages

SOLUTION: TLS/DTLS ENCRYPTION

TLS (Transport Layer Security):
├─ For TCP connections (MQTT, HTTP)
├─ Encryption: AES-256 (industry standard)
├─ Authentication: Certificate-based
├─ Handshake:
│  1. Client & server negotiate cipher
│  2. Server sends certificate
│  3. Client verifies certificate
│  4. Shared encryption key established
│  5. All data encrypted from this point
├─ Overhead: Minimal (< 5% bandwidth)
└─ Latency: Handshake adds 50-100ms first connection

DTLS (Datagram TLS):
├─ For UDP connections (lightweight IoT)
├─ Similar to TLS but for UDP
├─ Used by: CoAP protocol
├─ Better for: Constrained devices
└─ Examples: Very low-power sensors

HTTPS EXAMPLE (HTTP over TLS):
```
HTTP Request (unencrypted):
└─ GET /api/sensor/temperature
   └─ Visible to anyone sniffing network

HTTPS Request (TLS encrypted):
└─ TLS Handshake (negotiates encryption)
   GET /api/sensor/temperature
   └─ Encrypted: [garbled bytes] - unreadable
```

MQTT with TLS EXAMPLE:

```python
import paho.mqtt.client as mqtt

# WITHOUT TLS (Insecure):
client = mqtt.Client()
client.connect("192.168.1.100", 1883)  # Port 1883 (unencrypted)
client.publish("sensors/temp", "22.5")  # Data visible

# WITH TLS (Secure):
client = mqtt.Client()
client.tls_set(
    ca_certs="ca.crt",
    certfile="client.crt",
    keyfile="client.key"
)
client.connect("broker.example.com", 8883)  # Port 8883 (encrypted)
client.publish("sensors/temp", "22.5")  # Data encrypted
```

ENCRYPTION LEVELS:

Level 1: No Encryption (Worst):
├─ Data visible in plaintext
├─ Anyone can intercept
├─ Use: Only for non-sensitive test data
└─ Example: Home WiFi sensor on local network only

Level 2: Network Encryption (Better):
├─ WiFi encryption (WPA2)
├─ Data encrypted within network
├─ Vulnerable outside network
├─ Use: Local networks (home, office)
└─ Example: Local MQTT broker with WPA2

Level 3: Application Encryption (Best):
├─ TLS encryption
├─ Data encrypted even on local network
├─ Secure over any network
├─ Use: Cloud transmission, sensitive data
└─ Example: MQTT over TLS to cloud broker

Level 4: End-to-End Encryption (Ultimate):
├─ Data encrypted on device before sending
├─ Only recipient can decrypt
├─ Even broker cannot see data
├─ Use: Ultra-sensitive data (medical, finance)
└─ Example: Device encrypts with recipient's public key

PERFORMANCE IMPACT:

Device with TLS:
├─ Memory: +10-20KB (for certificates)
├─ CPU: +5-10% during encryption
├─ Latency: +50-100ms (first connection handshake)
├─ Latency: +0ms (subsequent packets, negligible overhead)
├─ Battery: -5% (encryption overhead)
└─ Verdict: Acceptable trade-off

Typical MQTT Packet:
├─ Without TLS: Plaintext, human-readable
├─ With TLS: Binary encrypted, random-looking

BEST PRACTICES:
├─ Always use TLS for cloud communication
├─ Use TLS for local if data sensitive
├─ Combine with authentication (certificates)
├─ Update certificates before expiration
├─ Monitor for suspicious patterns
└─ Rotate encryption keys periodically
```

